{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Internship Progress Update",
  "variants": [
    {
      "label": "Progress Update",
      "body": "Hi,

Quick update on the dog emotion classifier project:

We identified and resolved a major data leakage issue in the Anxiety class — the dataset had 600 files that were all chunks from a single source recording, plus augmented duplicates, which was causing artificial 100% accuracy. After source-level deduplication and cleaning, we're now at a clean, leakage-free dataset across all 5 classes (Aggressive: 620, Alert: 826, Anxiety: 632, Bored: 240, Playful: 477).

We've also rebuilt the entire training pipeline from scratch using HuBERT + RandomForest with a source-aware GroupShuffleSplit to ensure zero leakage between train and validation sets. Currently switching from HuBERT to WavLM (microsoft/wavlm-base-plus) as the feature extractor for better performance on non-human audio.

One thing I wanted to check with you — how should we handle howling sounds? From what I've researched, dog howling can indicate multiple emotions (Anxiety, Playful, or Alert depending on context). Should we include them under a specific class, split them based on context, or exclude them for now to keep the labels clean?

Thanks"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Reply to manager about 100% accuracy",
  "variants": [
    {
      "label": "Reply",
      "body": "The 100% accuracy was with neither \u2014 it was a data leakage issue from before. The model was trained on data where chunks from the same source recording appeared in both train and validation sets, so it was essentially memorizing the data rather than learning.\n\nAfter fixing the leakage, the first clean run with HuBERT gave 30% accuracy, which is expected given it's a human speech model. We're now switching to WavLM which is better suited for non-human audio \u2014 results will be ready soon."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Accuracy on unseen data reply",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "30.7% with HuBERT on truly unseen data. Currently retraining with WavLM which should improve this significantly.",
      "label": "Short reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Reply about howling and accuracy target",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Understood sir. I'll classify howling based on duration, pitch and sound intensity as you suggested.\n\nCurrently retraining with WavLM \u2014 will update you on the accuracy once done. Will aim for 65-70%.",
      "label": "Reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "82.5% accuracy achieved",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Hi sir,\n\nGreat news! After merging the classes as you suggested, the model hit 82.5% accuracy on unseen data \u2014 well above the 65-70% target.\n\nHigh Arousal (Aggressive + Alert): F1 = 0.89\nLow Arousal (Bored + Anxiety): F1 = 0.88\nPlayful: F1 = 0.37 (still weak, overlaps with other classes)\n\nPlayful is the only class still struggling. Should I work on improving that next, or move to the next stage?",
      "label": "Update"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Confidence threshold reply",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "No sir, we haven't set any confidence threshold yet. The 81-82.5% accuracy includes all predictions regardless of confidence score.\n\nWe could add a threshold (e.g., only accept predictions above 70% confidence, otherwise mark as \"uncertain\") which would likely push accuracy higher on the accepted predictions, but would mean some audio clips don't get classified. Should I implement this?",
      "label": "Reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Confidence threshold details",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Sir, here are the exact numbers:\n\nThe 81% accuracy is at 0% threshold (accepting all predictions). Average confidence across all predictions is 80.8%.\n\nIf we add a confidence threshold:\n- At 70% threshold: 90.3% accuracy, but only 77.5% of clips get classified (rest marked uncertain)\n- At 80% threshold: 93.1% accuracy, covering 60.9% of clips\n- At 90% threshold: 93.4% accuracy, covering only 38.9% of clips\n\nSo there's a clear tradeoff \u2014 higher threshold = higher accuracy but fewer clips actually get classified. For the app, I'd suggest 70% as a good balance (90% accuracy, still classifies 3 out of 4 clips). Let me know what threshold you'd like to go with.",
      "label": "Detailed reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Confidence threshold details - simple",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Sir, the 81% accuracy is when we accept every prediction the model makes, no matter how confident it is. On average the model is 80.8% confident across all predictions.\n\nIf we only trust predictions above a certain confidence level, accuracy goes up but we classify fewer clips:\n\n70% confidence cutoff \u2192 90.3% accuracy, but 22.5% of clips get skipped as \"unsure\"\n80% confidence cutoff \u2192 93.1% accuracy, but 39% of clips get skipped\n90% confidence cutoff \u2192 93.4% accuracy, but 61% of clips get skipped\n\nBasically it's a tradeoff \u2014 the more confident we require the model to be, the more accurate it is, but the more clips it just refuses to classify. I'd suggest going with 70% as a balance, since we still classify most clips (77.5%) at a much higher accuracy. Let me know what you think.",
      "label": "Simple reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Playful class low accuracy reply",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Yes sir, Playful is the weakest class at ~46% precision and recall. The main reasons are:\n\n1. Less data compared to other classes (only 477 files vs 1446 for High Arousal)\n2. Playful barks sound acoustically similar to Alert barks, so the model confuses them\n\nTo improve Playful, we would need more diverse and verified Playful dog sound data. Should I focus on collecting more Playful data, or should we move forward with the current model and implement the Neutral fallback threshold first?",
      "label": "Reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Question for manager",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Sir one quick question before I start building \u2014 for the environmental sound detector, should it run continuously in the background all the time, or only when a bark is detected?\n\nAsking because if it runs only after a bark, we might miss sounds that happened before the bark like a door closing. But if it runs all the time it will use more processing power.",
      "label": "Question"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Environmental classifier done",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Hi sir,

Done with the environmental sound classifier. Collected data from ESC-50, UrbanSound8K and Freesound — around 4400 audio files across 10 classes (door slam, doorbell, footsteps, siren, speech, scratching, alarm, dog bark external, silence, background noise).

Trained a simple RandomForest classifier on it and got 89% accuracy.

Now we have both models ready:
- Dog emotion model: 80.5% accuracy
- Environmental sound model: 89% accuracy

Shall I now build the scenario engine that combines both to detect the 5 triggers?",
      "label": "Update"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Unseen data accuracy reply",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Yes sir, the 89% is on unseen data. We did an 80/20 split \u2014 trained on 3520 files and tested on 881 files that the model had never seen. So 89% is the real accuracy on unseen data.",
      "label": "Reply"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "kind": "other",
  "summary_title": "Pipeline building reply",
  "variants": [
    {
      "body": "Sure sir, starting on it. Will build the complete pipeline combining both models, find test audio for all 5 scenarios, and include both positive and negative test cases. Will update you once done.",
      "label": "Reply"
    }
  ]
}

